-- Conformance: a `data` name the compiler special-cases is reserved (Chapter 8, Section 8.4.1)
-- Tests: E158 — `data Future` is rejected at check (#1397).
-- The prelude's data types (Option, Result, Ordering, UrlParts) stay
-- declarable: a program may shadow those, and §8.4.1 says so. `Future`
-- and `Tuple` are the two whose SEMANTICS codegen recognises by name —
-- how a value is rendered, compared and laid out — so a declaration of
-- either could not be told apart from the built-in. Accepting `Future`
-- compiled to a module that fails to load; `Tuple` is the sibling
-- fixture ch08_builtin_tuple_redefinition_rejected.
private data Future {
  MkShadow(Int)
}

public fn main(-> @Int)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  0
}
